diff --git a/pages/cdn64.en.html b/pages/cdn64.en.html
index 05f7c6c..7033e84 100644
--- a/pages/cdn64.en.html
+++ b/pages/cdn64.en.html
@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@
 <p>In your home network, the local reverse proxy of Node64 is called <strong>PROXY64</strong>; <strong>CDN64</strong> is the server part at IPv64.net. You manage CDN64 at <a href="https://ipv64.net/cdn64" rel="noopener">ipv64.net/cdn64</a>, in Node64 and via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}">CDN64 API</a>.</p>
 
 <h2 id="funktionsweise">How CDN64 works</h2>
-<p>A name you switch on in CDN64 (e.g. <code>cloud.deinedomain.ipv64.net</code>) points to the edge servers of your account in DNS – your account class decides how many (see <a href="#server-je-konto">servers per account</a>). A visitor lands on one of the servers. The server recognises from the name where the connection belongs and passes it on.</p>
+<p>A name you switch on in CDN64 (e.g. <code>cloud.deinedomain.ipv64.net</code>) points to the edge servers of its tunnel in DNS – your account class decides how many (see <a href="#server-je-tunnel">servers per tunnel</a>). A visitor lands on one of the servers. The server recognises from the name where the connection belongs and passes it on.</p>
 <pre class="ascii nocopy" aria-label="Diagram: visitors reach the edge servers, Node64 keeps a tunnel to each, in mode 3 the servers go directly to the target">
  Visitor (IPv4/IPv6)
         │  DNS: A/AAAA of your account's edge servers (TTL 10 s)
@@ -40,13 +40,14 @@
 	<li><strong>Parallel connections:</strong> depending on your account class, Node64 opens several parallel tunnel connections to each server (Business: 2, Company: 4, see <a href="#kontingent">quota</a>). The servers hand new visitors to the least loaded connection.</li>
 	<li><strong>Several tunnels for one name:</strong> a name can be assigned to several tunnels (i.e. several Node64 instances). The order is the priority: the first tunnel that is currently connected serves the name. This is a fallback, not load balancing between nodes. You set the list via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#name-aendern">API</a> (field <code>tunnels</code>).</li>
 </ul>
-<h3 id="server-je-konto">Servers per account</h3>
-<p>Not every account uses all edge servers. Your account class decides how many: Free 1, Home / Supporter 2, Business 1 or 2 (your choice), Company 1 to all (your choice; “all” includes future servers). The number applies to all names of the account and to all modes, including mode 3.</p>
+<h3 id="server-je-tunnel">Servers per tunnel</h3>
+<p>Not every tunnel uses all edge servers. Your account class decides how many: Free 1, Home / Supporter 2, Business 1 or 2 (your choice), Company 1 to all (your choice; “all” includes future servers). The number applies per Node64 tunnel to all names of that tunnel. Names in mode 3 (without a tunnel) use the highest number of the class.</p>
 <ul>
-	<li><strong>Assignment:</strong> IPv64.net assigns the servers automatically – one per location first, each time the one with the fewest accounts. The assignment stays fixed until the number changes. ipv64.net/cdn64 shows the servers your account currently uses in the <em>Servers</em> line.</li>
-	<li><strong>Choosing the number (Business, Company):</strong> on ipv64.net/cdn64 in the <em>Servers</em> line or via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#server">API</a>. IPv64.net then updates the DNS of all names; Node64 opens its tunnels to the new servers within a minute.</li>
-	<li><strong>Class changed:</strong> the new number applies within a few minutes; servers that remain are the ones assigned first.</li>
-	<li><strong>Old DNS answer at the visitor:</strong> if a visitor still reaches a server your account no longer uses, that server passes them on to a server with your tunnel.</li>
+	<li><strong>Assignment:</strong> IPv64.net assigns the servers automatically – one per location first, each time the one with the fewest tunnels. The assignment stays fixed until the number changes. Node64 shows the servers a tunnel currently uses in the <em>CDN64</em> column of the proxy hosts.</li>
+	<li><strong>Choosing the number (Business, Company):</strong> in Node64 under <em>PROXY64 → Proxy hosts</em> in the <em>CDN64</em> column (button with the number of servers) or via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#server">API</a>, per tunnel. IPv64.net then updates the DNS of the names of this tunnel; Node64 connects to the new servers within a minute, connections to the remaining ones keep running.</li>
+	<li><strong>Several tunnels for one name:</strong> DNS names the servers of the first tunnel. The backup tunnels additionally connect to these servers so they can take over.</li>
+	<li><strong>Class changed:</strong> the new number applies within a few minutes; the tunnel keeps the servers assigned first.</li>
+	<li><strong>Old DNS answer at the visitor:</strong> if a visitor still reaches a server the tunnel no longer uses, that server passes them on to a server with your tunnel.</li>
 </ul>
 
 <h2 id="modi">The three modes</h2>
@@ -211,7 +212,7 @@
