@@ -309,7 +305,7 @@ curl -X DELETE "https://ipv64.net/api/v2/cdn64o/hosts/42/mappings/7" -H "Authori
 	<li><code>DELETE /cdn64o/tunnels/{id}</code> removes the tunnel (<code>204</code>). Names that are no longer assigned to any other tunnel are switched off – DNS back, target, access list and port mappings removed. If the DNS server rejects this, you get <code>502</code> (<code>dns_update_failed</code>); tunnel and name stay so you can try again later.</li>
 </ul>
 <p id="tunnel-listen"><code>GET</code> and <code>PUT /cdn64o/tunnels/{id}/lists</code> read and set the block list (<code>deny</code>) and the allow list (<code>allow</code>) of the tunnel: IP addresses and networks (IPv4/IPv6, CIDR), at most 1000 per list (otherwise <code>422</code>, <code>list_too_long</code>). Entries are stored normalised, IPv4-mapped networks as IPv4; addresses with a zone give <code>422</code>. The edge servers reject addresses on the block list for all names of the tunnel right at connection setup; the allow list takes precedence. <code>PUT</code> replaces the lists sent, a missing field stays. Node64 keeps both lists in line with the lists under <em>PROXY64 → Access lists</em>.</p>
-<p>The tunnel object (also in <code>GET /cdn64o</code>) contains <code>id</code> (e.g. <code>tn_…</code>), <code>name</code>, <code>node_id</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>created_at</code>, <code>last_seen</code> (last connection to an edge server), <code>last_edge</code>, <code>node_version</code> and <code>sessions</code> (parallel connections per server according to the account class).</p>
+<p>The tunnel object (also in <code>GET /cdn64o</code>) contains <code>id</code> (e.g. <code>tn_…</code>), <code>name</code>, <code>node_id</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>created_at</code>, <code>last_seen</code> (last connection to an edge server), <code>last_edge</code>, <code>node_version</code>, <code>sessions</code> (parallel connections per server according to the account class), <code>edges</code> (the servers the tunnel connects to) and <code>servers</code> (assignment, see <a href="#server">edge servers of a tunnel</a>).</p>
 <pre><code>curl -X DELETE "https://ipv64.net/api/v2/cdn64o/tunnels/tn_DEINE_TUNNEL_ID" -H "Authorization: Bearer DEIN_API_KEY"</code></pre>
 <div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-title">Protect the token</div><p>With the tunnel token a node can log in to the edge servers as your tunnel. Do not share it. If it has leaked, issue a new one.</p></div>
 
